# Build context trim for `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .` (root context, whole tree).
#
# docker/Dockerfile deliberately does `COPY . .` rather than naming pom.xml and src/: a file list
# has to be edited every time the repo gains top-level content the build needs, and forgetting is
# silent. This file is what makes that affordable — it drops everything the build does not read, so
# adding a top-level entry stays free and no stale output leaks in.

# `**/.git` rather than `.git`, so a future submodule's .git FILE goes too. Neither is readable from
# the build stage and neither is read by it.
**/.git
.gitignore

# Maven / Quarkus build output — regenerated inside the build stage. Copying it in would also risk a
# stale target/ shadowing what the stage compiles.
**/target
**/target-ide

# The client's install output. Hundreds of megabytes of symlink farm, and the build stage reads
# none of it — it runs no install and no client build.
#
# NOTE THE ASYMMETRY with the platform's recipe, which excludes dist/ too: here dist/ is the
# PAYLOAD. It is the bundle the CI step built where the @qits registry is reachable, it is the only
# form of the client this image can be given, and excluding it would fail the build at the test -f.
src/main/webui/node_modules
src/main/webui/.angular

# Local env / IDE cruft
.env
.idea
.vscode
.sdkmanrc

# Docs and the docker/ dir itself: nothing in the build reads them, and keeping them out means
# editing this Dockerfile or a README does not bust the `COPY . .` layer.
*.md
docker
